Nintendo will stop allowing users to sign in to their Nintendo Account with an Apple or Google account on January 26, 2027, the company has <a href="https://www.nintendo.com/au/support/articles/discontinuation-of-google-and-apple-accounts-linking-to-nintendo-accounts/">announced</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/sign-in-with-apple.jpeg" alt="" width="1200" height="675" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1077071" /><br/>
Users will also no longer be able to create a Nintendo Account through either service. Nintendo has not explained why it is removing the options, but notes that the exact cutoff could be delayed depending on the progress of its system updates.<br/>
<br/>
After the change, users will need to sign in with their Nintendo Account email address or Sign-In ID and password, or use a passkey. Nintendo says users who are already signed in on a device may not need to sign in again immediately.<br/>
<br/>
Anyone who usually signs in through Apple or Google should check that they know their Nintendo Account login details before the deadline. Those who have never set a password, or have forgotten it, can select "Forgot your password?" on the <a href="https://accounts.nintendo.com/">Nintendo Account login page</a> to request a reset email.<br/>
<br/>
Users who chose to hide their email address with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/sign-in-with-apple/">Sign in with Apple</a> may have an Apple-generated relay address registered with Nintendo. Messages sent to that address are forwarded to their personal inbox. Nintendo advises anyone who can no longer access their registered email address to contact its support team.<br/>
<br/>
